# Storage recovery capabilities

## Required outcome

Recovery must preserve the original bytes and a newer writer's output. It must
retain every needed recovery copy when an operation refuses or its outcome is
ambiguous. Automatic rollback restoration is qualified only where storage binds
the comparison to replacement or enforces exclusive custody across writers.
Otherwise the unresolved state and missing capability remain visible.

This is the continuation assigned against root
`146ca1e83594692d256ada57de05026754883c7f`, tracked in private issue 133.
Local/synthetic verification is authorized; live resources, new services,
downloads, installation and publication are outside this work.

## Inspected baseline and primary references

The storage/recovery source at lane base `0c8846542` matches the named root.
At that baseline, `BlobAttachmentStore` delegates to unconditional BlobDriver writes.
The filesystem adapter writes a unique temporary file and renames over the
destination. That preserves a whole file but does not compare the destination
at replacement; raw filesystem and Ruby writers do not honor a JavaScript lock.

The [R2 Workers binding reference](https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/api/workers/workers-api-reference/)
documents destination ETag conditions on PUT and a null result when a condition
refuses the operation. The body and ETag must come from the same GET object;
separate HEAD and body reads would not bind the verified bytes to that receipt.

The [configured Bunny HTTP API specification](https://docs.bunny.net/api-reference/storage/openapi.json)
lists authentication and an incoming-payload checksum for uploads, without a
documented destination write precondition. Payload integrity is not a compare
against the existing destination. This does not establish that every Bunny API
rejects such conditions; it means this configured interface has no verified
conditional replacement contract. Both primary documents were retrieved through
the search gateway; a generated search summary was not accepted as evidence.

## Implemented capability and remaining limitation

`BlobAttachmentStore.restoreIfUnchanged` translates the same single-object
files/tmp keys as its ordinary writes, then delegates only to the selected
driver's conditional operation. Coded and multiple-rendition operations refuse;
there is no atomic multi-object promise.

The memory driver's private Map is its storage substrate. Its byte comparison
and copied replacement run synchronously without an await or virtual put call.
This qualifies that actual memory store, not an unrelated filesystem protected
by an instance-local mutex.

R2 reads the expected bytes and ETag from the same object snapshot and sends
`onlyIf.etagMatches` with PUT. A null reply means refusal. A rejected write or
invalid acknowledgement raises `blob-conditional-write-outcome-unknown` with
`retrySafe: false`; no mutation is retried. Recovery retains every preimage on
any refusal or uncertainty, including a partially restored set.

This interface compares bytes. It does not promise upload-epoch identity:
ETags are not unique upload versions, so an identical-byte rewrite or an ABA
sequence ending in identical bytes is not distinguished. Metadata-only newer
writes are not protected by a byte-comparison contract. Hosted R2 acceptance is
not established by the local workerd tests.

Filesystem and the configured Bunny HTTP driver expose precise capability
reasons through `blob-conditional-write-unavailable`, with `retrySafe: false`.
They do not implement unconditional read/check/write recovery. Their ordinary
storage operations are unchanged. Automatic rollback on these stores remains
unresolved; the replacement and backup remain available for reconciliation.

## Architectural investigation for filesystem and Bunny

Filesystem rename is an unconditional destination replacement. An advisory
lock, a driver-local queue, a lock-file convention, or a configuration promise
does not bind existing raw filesystem/Ruby writers. A filesystem solution must
first establish either an OS-enforced destination comparison primitive valid
on the supported platforms, or a storage design with immutable objects and an
atomically conditional authoritative pointer. The latter requires all writers
and serving paths, including Ruby, to participate; it is not a local adapter
patch. No such primitive or migration was established or introduced here.

For Bunny, the next qualifying evidence would be a documented destination
conditional replacement primitive on the actual configured interface, or an
all-writer storage migration with equivalent custody. An incoming checksum,
an undocumented If-Match header, or a successful sequential mock PUT does not
provide that evidence. No live provider probe or new service was authorized.

