## Issue 134: owned Buffer snapshots

Independent review at `ba29dacea` found that defensive instance `.slice()`
calls alias Node Buffer memory. Buffer is a valid Uint8Array input. Reusing
the caller's Buffer after a memory PUT changed stored bytes; reusing expected
or replacement buffers during R2 GET changed the submitted comparison or
replacement. Native ETag enforcement cannot correct a comparison that the
adapter itself allowed to change.

The four independent tests from review commit `f8077547c` were copied without
changing their assertions into `tests/server/conditional-blob-buffer-ownership.test.ts`.
All four failed against `ba29dacea`; the raw failing JUnit result is retained
in the lane's ignored `.runtime/issue134-baseline-red.xml`. The repair uses
`new Uint8Array(bytes)` at the memory storage boundary, R2 comparison and
replacement boundary, BlobAttachmentStore delegation, and recovery helper's
borrowed backup/current reads. This takes owned bytes for Buffer inputs as
well as ordinary Uint8Array inputs, before asynchronous validation.

The four original attacks then passed unchanged. Two further tests cover
borrowed backup Buffer mutation after validation and borrowed target Buffer
mutation at conditional replacement. All 153 tests in the prior selected
storage/recovery group plus the independent four-case file passed. Typecheck
and diff checks passed; the explicit six-file platform scan found zero
unguarded command hits. No database save or conditional mutation is retried.
Filesystem/Bunny, upload-epoch and hosted acceptance limits above remain open.

The lead identified the same assumption at CardActionEngine's original read,
recovery-copy and rollback-before-image boundaries. An actual save test with
a custom store returning a Buffer confirmed it: after partial replacement
failed, database state rolled back but the old engine restored the mutated
replacement rather than the original bytes. That failing result is retained
in `.runtime/issue134-engine-baseline-red.xml`. The engine now snapshots the
borrowed read immediately and owns separate recovery and rollback arrays.
Its surgical delta changes only these copies. The actual save test then
passed, bringing the final selected eleven-file run to 154 passed, zero
skipped. These runs are successive evidence, not additive distinct-test totals.
